# Tangerine Compiler — Target Descriptions
# Self-hosting compiler written in Tangerine
#
# AUDIT P1-14, slices 1 + 16 — the DATA-DRIVEN TARGET DESCRIPTION.
#
# CONTRACT: every target property a backend can need — pointer width,
# endianness, stack alignment, the ATOMIC-WIDTH CLASSES (native vs
# emulated vs MMIO-safe), unaligned-access behavior, hardware
# divide, the per-CPU ISA/FPU split (arch/cpu/isa/isa_features/
# float_abi/fpu — the CPU and the FPU are SEPARATE data: a Cortex-M4
# without its optional FPU is NOT the same target as a Cortex-M4F),
# the calling-convention register classes (arg / caller-saved /
# callee-saved), the interrupt (handler) ABI, and the flash/RAM-style
# address spaces — is DATA: one canonical TargetDesc instance per
# target, defined in THIS module and consumed by the backends (the LIR
# backend's accessor is lir_target_desc() in lir.tg, which the
# driver's LIR route consults at its gate). Target properties must NOT
# be re-derived as scattered arch conditionals inside codegen: a
# backend that needs a target property reads it from the desc it is
# given. Hardcoded ABI numbers that predate this module (e.g. lir.tg's
# register-model constants — fixed x0-x7, pool x9-x14, scratch
# x15/x16/x17) are LEGACY READS of the same data; they migrate onto
# desc fields slice by slice, never with a behavior change in one
# slice.
#
# AUDIT §16 (this slice) — WHAT CHANGED, DATA-SHAPE WISE:
#   - the single cortex_m_desc instance (which conflated the M3/M4
#     class with a VFP unit every core carries) is REPLACED by four
#     per-CPU instances: thumbv7m_cortex_m3, thumbv7em_cortex_m4,
#     thumbv7em_cortex_m4f and thumbv7em_cortex_m7. The CPU, the ISA
#     and the FPU are separate fields: a Cortex-M3 or a bare
#     Cortex-M4 has fpu = None and float_abi = Soft and must NOT
#     receive VFP instructions, while the M4F/M7 hard-float
#     instances carry fpu = FPv4SP/FPv5D16 and the AAPCS32 VFP
#     (hard-float) crossing rules. The ARMv7-M integer divide
#     (SDIV/UDIV) IS present on M3/M4/M7 (the pre-slice comment
#     claiming M3 lacks it was wrong; the M0/M0+ ARMv6-M class lacks
#     it — RuntimeHelper there).
#   - the single atomic_widths list is replaced by the three width
#     CLASSES: native_atomic_widths (true hardware atomic
#     instructions), emulated_atomic_widths (widths served by the
#     masked word-width RMW emulation of audit item 43 — see the
#     authority paragraph below) and mmio_atomic_widths
#     (widths safe against MMIO side registers — an EMULATED subword
#     RMW via a word LR/SC may touch the neighbouring register and is
#     therefore never MMIO-safe). RISC-V: RV32IMA/RV64IMA without
#     Zabha have native [4] / [4, 8], emulated [1, 2] and mmio ==
#     native; Zabha moves [1, 2] into native (the token is documented
#     below, no Zabha instance is provided in this slice).
#
# AUDIT ITEM 39 (this slice) — THE ONE TRIPLE -> DESC LOOKUP, and the
# end of the ENV-ONLY TARGET IDENTITY: the --target triples select the
# descriptor instances through ONE table — target_desc_of_triple below —
# which maps every canonical instance by its canonical --target triple
# (aarch64-unknown-none, thumbv7m-none-eabi -> cortex-m3, thumbv7em-
# none-eabi -> cortex-m4, thumbv7em-none-eabihf -> cortex-m4f, the
# riscv32imac/imafc/imafdc-unknown-none-elf and riscv64imac/riscv64gc-
# unknown-none-elf triples -> the riscv instances) plus the host triples
# and the LEGACY route names as alias keys of the SAME table (cortex-m3
# ... cortex-m7, cortex-m (-> m4f), riscv32/riscv64 (-> the imac
# instances of their xlen), aarch64). There is no second target-naming
# system: the driver's LIR route resolves its backend from this table
# (the --target triple when it names a thumb/riscv desc; the
# TANGERINE_LIR_TARGET env value only as a legacy alias through the same
# table) — see driver.tg's compile_to_embedded_route / compile_lir_route.
# Triples with an embedded spec but NO descriptor instance (thumbv6m-none-
# eabi, thumbv8m.main-none-eabihf, riscv32imc-unknown-none-elf) stay
# fail-closed: no desc, no codegen, the stable rejection. The
# cortex-m7 instance has no canonical rustc-style triple (the
# thumbv7em-none-eabihf triple canonicalizes to the m4f instance — the
# least-breaking mapping: the FPv4-SP subset is the common denominator of
# the hard-float M-profile class); cortex-m7 is reached by its desc-name
# key only, and per-CPU variance beyond the triple table is NOT a
# separate --cpu/--features naming system this slice (the decision is
# reported: per-CPU variance is carried by the triple table, whose
# canonical instances are the compiler's target universe).
#
# AUDIT ITEM 43 (this slice) — THE ONE ATOMIC AUTHORITY: every width/
# op/order decision of the atomic consumers is made by asking ONE
# target-side question — target_desc.atomic_support(desc, width,
# op_kind, order_word) -> AtomicSupport (Native | Emulated |
# Unsupported) over the width classes below, with the MMIO legality a
# SEPARATE question (mmio_atomic_support over mmio_atomic_widths). The
# consumers that route through it are the LIR atomic-call lowering
# (lir.tg's lir_lower_atomic_call — the route width caps), the
# ARMv7-M validator (backend_thumb.tg's thumb_validate_ptr_stmt — the
# 8-byte fail-closed), the RISC-V surface (backend_rv.tg's
# rv_validate_ptr_stmt AND rv_emit_atom_rmw/rv_emit_atom_cas — whose
# EMULATED-class sub-word widths are now served by the masked
# word-width LR/SC loops of this slice on NORMAL memory, never on
# MMIO), and the direct emitter's aarch64/x86 dispatch (codegen.tg's
# emit_atomic_intrinsic). No width rule is re-derived as a bare
# arch/xlen conditional anywhere; the class lists are the data, and
# the two authority functions are the only queries.
#
# AUDIT ITEM 34 (this slice) — THE ONE VECTOR-LEGALITY AUTHORITY: the
# vector FACILITY of a target is likewise data — vector_support(desc,
# bits) -> VectorSupport (V128 | V256 | Unsupported) over desc.fpu
# (AdvSimdA64 / Sse2 are the modeled 128-bit facilities) and the
# desc.isa_features "avx"/"avx2" tokens (the 256-bit opt-in). The LIR
# vector class (lir.tg's `vector_widths`/`vector_lanes` tables) and the
# explicit SIMD intrinsic lowering consult it; a 256-bit request on a
# desc without the AVX tokens and every 512-bit request fail closed
# with the target reason. See the VectorSupport section below.
#   - TargetDesc grows arch/cpu/isa/isa_features/float_abi/fpu/
#     fpu_register_width/integer_div/unaligned_policy/cache_line_size/
#     red_zone/tls_model/interrupt_abi (see the struct).
#   - interrupt_abi: the CORTEX-M NVIC handler convention is now
#     modeled data (the hardware's entry auto-stack, the EXC_RETURN
#     exit rule and the registers reserved across a handler body).
#     Other targets leave it None (their handler conventions are not
#     modeled yet — never silently assumed).
#   - vector_fpu/has_atomics/has_hardware_mul/unaligned_loads_ok are
#     GONE: the FPU set lives in fpu/float_abi/fpu_register_width,
#     the atomic surface in the three width classes, hardware
#     multiply in the ISA tokens / integer_div, and unaligned
#     behavior in unaligned_policy. No consumer outside this module
#     read those fields (verified at this slice's implementation).
#
# NOT YET MODELED (documented here, never silently assumed):
#   - integer ABI: no separate field — for every canonical instance
#     the integer word is exactly pointer_width (LP64 on aarch64/
#     x86_64/riscv64, ILP32 on cortex_m/riscv32). A target that
#     diverges (x32, ILP32 aarch64) will grow an int_abi field.
#   - vector/FPU granularity beyond the modeled FPU units: the
#     instances document the exact assumed feature set (see each
#     block below). The FPv4-SP unit of the Cortex-M4F models the
#     vfpv4-d16 double-REGISTER file (d0..d15) with the FPv4-SP
#     single-precision data-processing subset; double-precision VFP
#     data processing (the F64 register-resident forms this compiler's
#     float slice emits) is FPv5-D16-class and rides the Cortex-M7
#     instance's "vfp-dp" isa token. The vector FACILITY is modeled
#     since audit item 34 (vector_support: the 128-bit AdvSimdA64/Sse2
#     units and the 256-bit avx/avx2 tokens); what stays unmodeled is
#     vector-unit granularity beyond those widths (SVE/SME, AVX-512 —
#     no descriptor carries a token for them, so they answer
#     Unsupported and fail closed).
#   - TLS: tls_model is "none" on every instance; a target whose
#     runtime enables TLS grows the model token here.
#
# The instances are plain functions returning FRESH records — the desc
# is value data, never a shared mutable singleton, so two consumers (or
# two phases of one compile) can never observe each other's mutations.

use std::core::{Option}
use std::collections::{Vec}

# ———————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————
# Endianness
# ———————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————

enum Endianness
  Little
  Big
end

# ———————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————
# Arch — the machine-word / instruction-profile family
# ———————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————

enum Arch
  Armv6M    # Cortex-M0/M0+ (Thumb-1; the word-only exclusive class)
  Armv7M    # Cortex-M3/M4/M7 (Thumb-2)
  AArch64
  X86_64
  RiscV32
  RiscV64
end

# ———————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————
# Cpu — the modeled core. Generic members for the non-embedded
# profiles (their per-SoC variation is not a CPU-class difference for
# this compiler); the Cortex-M cores are per-core because the FPU and
# the divide unit are OPTIONAL per core and the class is what the
# backend compiles for.
# ———————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————

enum Cpu
  CortexM0
  CortexM0Plus
  CortexM3
  CortexM4
  CortexM4F
  CortexM7
  GenericAArch64
  GenericX86_64
  GenericRiscV32
  GenericRiscV64
end

# ———————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————
# FloatAbi — the FLOAT CALLING CONVENTION (which register file a
# float argument/return crosses in), independent of whether an FPU
# exists:
#   Soft    — no FP instructions, no FP ABI registers (base
#             convention: float values ride the general registers /
#             stack as their base-type words). A CPU with fpu None is
#             ALWAYS Soft in this module's instances.
#   SoftFp  — FP instructions allowed, base ABI discipline (not used
#             by any instance this slice; a future fp-softfp instance
#             would carry it).
#   Hard    — the FP ABI register file is in use (AAPCS32 VFP on the
#             M-profile hard-float instances: F32/F64 arguments at FP
#             positions 0..7 cross in s0..s15 / d0..d7, returns in
#             s0/d0).
# ———————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————

enum FloatAbi
  Soft
  SoftFp
  Hard
end

# ———————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————
# Fpu — the modeled FPU/vector unit. fpu_register_width carries the
# register-file width in bits (FLEN on RISC-V, 128 for the AArch64
# v-registers / SSE2, 64 for the M-profile double-register files).
# ———————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————

enum Fpu
  None
  FPv4SP    # Cortex-M4F: vfpv4-d16, single-precision data processing
  FPv5D16   # Cortex-M7: vfpv5-d16, double-precision data processing
  AdvSimdA64
  Sse2
  RVF       # RISC-V F extension (FLEN = 32)
  RVD       # RISC-V F+D extensions (FLEN = 64)
  # Cortex-M7 single-precision option: vfpv5-d16, SP data processing
  # only (same single-precision discipline as FPv4SP; appended so the
  # pre-existing variant tags do not shift).
  FPv5SP
end

# ———————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————
# target_desc_f64_data_processing — the ONE scalar double-precision
# data-processing capability (audit §16, the M-profile FPU variant
# gate): does the modeled FPU execute the DOUBLE-precision
# data-processing instruction class — F64 arithmetic (VADD/VSUB/VMUL/
# VDIV/VNEG/VABS), F64 compares (VCMP), the float-width conversions to
# or from D (VCVT.F32.F64 / VCVT.F64.F32) and the int<->double
# conversions (VCVT.F64.<int> / VCVT.<int>.F64)?
#   - FPv5D16 (Cortex-M7 double-precision option, the `vfp-dp` token):
#     TRUE — the full scalar F64 data-processing set.
#   - FPv4SP (Cortex-M4F): FALSE — FPv4-SP is single-precision only;
#     its double-register support is the load/store/transfer subset
#     (VLDR/VSTR/VPUSH/VPOP and the VMOV core<->D forms), plus the
#     M-profile d0..d15 register file. VMOV.F64 Dd, Dm, VCVT between
#     F32 and F64 and every F64 data-processing op are NOT in the
#     FPv4-SP instruction set.
#   - FPv5SP (the Cortex-M7 single-precision option): FALSE — the
#     FPv5-SP unit carries the same single-precision-only discipline.
#   - AdvSimdA64 / Sse2 / RVD: TRUE (their scalar F64 data processing
#     is part of the modeled facility); RVF (FLEN 32) and None: FALSE.
# Consumers FAIL CLOSED on a false answer with the target's rule; the
# helper never fails open (an FPU the enum does not model with full
# F64 data processing answers false).
# ———————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————
def target_desc_f64_data_processing(desc: TargetDesc) -> Bool
  match desc.fpu
  when Fpu::FPv5D16 then true
  when Fpu::AdvSimdA64 then true
  when Fpu::Sse2 then true
  when Fpu::RVD then true
  when _ then false
  end
end

# ———————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————
# IntegerDiv — where the machine's integer divide comes from.
# ARMv7-M (M3/M4/M7) has hardware SDIV/UDIV; ARMv6-M (M0/M0+) does
# not (a divide lowers to a runtime helper there).
# ———————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————

enum IntegerDiv
  None
  RuntimeHelper
  Hardware
end

# ———————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————
# UnalignedPolicy — what happens to a NON-atomic normal load/store
# at an unaligned address. (Atomic accesses must be naturally aligned
# on EVERY architecture regardless of this field; the MMIO note of
# the atomic classes is separate data.)
# ———————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————

enum UnalignedPolicy
  Allowed    # normal accesses may be unaligned (architecturally)
  Faulting   # a misaligned access raises an exception
end

# ———————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————
# InterruptAbi — the handler (ISR) convention of a target, modeled
# as data consumed by the interrupt-function slice (the ARMv7-M
# backend — backend_thumb.tg's interrupt ABI section — compiles
# @interrupt functions against the NVIC record below):
#   name            — the convention's canonical token ("nvic").
#   entry_auto_saved — the registers the HARDWARE already stacked /
#                     preserved by the time the handler body starts
#                     (the body need not save them itself; their
#                     entry values are recoverable from the stack
#                     frame the hardware built).
#   exit_rule       — the canonical return mechanism token.
#   reserved        — registers the handler body must not treat as
#                     free scratch for the body's whole span (e.g.
#                     LR, which holds the EXC_RETURN magic on
#                     Cortex-M — a call inside the handler clobbers
#                     it, so a compiled handler must re-establish it
#                     before the exit rule's branch).
# A TargetDesc with no modeled interrupt ABI carries
# interrupt_abi = Option::None — the absence is data too.
# ———————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————

struct InterruptAbi
  name: String
  entry_auto_saved: Vec[String]
  exit_rule: String
  reserved: Vec[String]
end

# The Cortex-M NVIC handler convention (the same shape on every
# ARMv7-M core of this module): the NVIC entry stacks the exception
# frame {r0-r3, r12, lr, pc, xpsr} on the current stack (MSP when the
# handler preempts thread mode — handler mode always runs on the main
# stack), the handler body runs in handler mode with LR holding the
# EXC_RETURN value 0xFFFFFFF1 (return to handler mode with MSP — the
# form the compiler's own handlers use), and the ISR returns with
# `bx lr` on that value. LR is therefore RESERVED across the body:
# any inner call clobbers it, so a compiled handler saves the
# EXC_RETURN image before the first call and restores it before the
# exit rule's branch (backend_thumb.tg's interrupt slice — the
# prologue's PUSH {r4-r10, lr} LR slot IS the holder). r0-r3, r12 and
# lr are caller-saved per AAPCS but their entry values are the
# hardware's stacked ones — entry_auto_saved lists them so a handler
# prologue need not re-save what the NVIC already stacked.
def armv7m_interrupt_abi() -> InterruptAbi
  InterruptAbi {
    name: "nvic",
    entry_auto_saved: Vec::from([
      "r0", "r1", "r2", "r3", "r12", "lr", "pc", "xpsr"
    ]),
    exit_rule: "bx lr with lr = exc_return (0xFFFFFFF1)",
    reserved: Vec::from(["lr"])
  }
end

# ———————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————
# TargetDesc — the canonical per-target ABI record
# ———————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————
# Fields:
#   name / pointer_width / endianness / stack_alignment — the word
#     model (as before).
#   arch / cpu / isa — the core: arch is the profile family, cpu the
#     core class, isa the canonical extension string ("armv7-m",
#     "rv64gc", ...).
#   isa_features — extension tokens the compiler ASSUMES beyond the
#     arch/cpu/fpu fields (single-sourced spelling, consumed by no
#     backend this slice; the tokens document, e.g., the RISC-V
#     "zabha" subword-AMO extension that would move [1,2] from the
#     emulated to the native atomic class).
#   float_abi / fpu / fpu_register_width — the FPU split (see the
#     enums). fpu_register_width is 0 when fpu is None.
#   integer_div — Hardware | RuntimeHelper | None.
#   native_atomic_widths / emulated_atomic_widths /
#     mmio_atomic_widths — the atomic width classes (see the module
#     header and the AtomicSupport authority section below; the
#     EMULATED widths are served by backend_rv.tg's masked word-width
#     RMW loops on NORMAL memory — audit item 43 — and are NEVER
#     MMIO-safe by construction, so they never appear in
#     mmio_atomic_widths and mmio_atomic_support answers Unsupported
#     there).
#   unaligned_policy / cache_line_size (per-SoC data, None when not
#     assumed) / red_zone (bytes below SP the ABI reserves;
#     0 = none) / tls_model ("none" on every instance this slice).
#   interrupt_abi — Option::None unless the target's handler
#     convention is modeled (see InterruptAbi).
#   reg_caller_saved / reg_callee_saved / arg_regs / memory_spaces —
#     as before.
struct TargetDesc
  name: String                     # canonical instance name ("cortex-m4f", ...)
  pointer_width: Int               # bytes per pointer / address
  endianness: Endianness           # Little | Big
  stack_alignment: Int             # bytes the stack pointer stays aligned to
  arch: Arch                       # profile family (Armv7M, RiscV64, ...)
  cpu: Cpu                         # core class (CortexM4F, GenericRiscV64, ...)
  isa: String                      # canonical ISA string ("armv7-m", "rv64gc")
  isa_features: Vec[String]        # extension tokens beyond arch/cpu/fpu
  float_abi: FloatAbi              # float calling convention (Soft|SoftFp|Hard)
  fpu: Fpu                         # the FPU/vector unit (None = no FPU)
  fpu_register_width: Int          # FP register-file width in bits (0 = none)
  integer_div: IntegerDiv          # where integer divide comes from
  native_atomic_widths: Vec[Int]   # byte widths with native atomic instructions
  emulated_atomic_widths: Vec[Int] # byte widths serveable by word RMW emulation
  mmio_atomic_widths: Vec[Int]     # widths safe for MMIO side registers
  unaligned_policy: UnalignedPolicy # non-atomic unaligned-access behavior
  cache_line_size: Option[Int] = Option::None   # per-SoC; None = not assumed
  red_zone: Int                    # ABI red-zone bytes below SP (0 = none)
  tls_model: String                # "none" on every instance this slice
  interrupt_abi: Option[InterruptAbi] = Option::None
  reg_caller_saved: Vec[String]    # registers a call may clobber
  reg_callee_saved: Vec[String]    # registers a callee must preserve
  arg_regs: Vec[String]            # integer/pointer argument registers, ABI order
  memory_spaces: Vec[String]       # address spaces ("flash", "ram", ...)
end

# ———————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————
# AtomicSupport — the ONE atomic authority's answer (audit item 43)
# ———————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————
# The target side of every atomic width/op/order decision a consumer
# makes. The three answers over a TargetDesc's atomic width classes:
#   Native      — the width has TRUE hardware atomic instructions on
#                 this target (it is in native_atomic_widths): the
#                 backend emits its instruction forms (LSE/load-store
#                 on aarch64, LDREX/STREX + DMB on ARMv7-M, the A
#                 extension's LR/SC on RISC-V).
#   Emulated    — the width has no atomic instruction, but the
#                 description's emulated_atomic_widths class admits a
#                 masked word-width RMW emulation (RISC-V sub-word
#                 1/2-byte RMW/CAS on NORMAL memory — backend_rv.tg's
#                 word-LR/SC loops, which read and write the WHOLE
#                 surrounding word; such an operation must NEVER be
#                 used on an MMIO register — see mmio_atomic_support).
#   Unsupported — the width is in no class for this target (or the
#                 order word is not a memory-model ordering): fail
#                 closed at the consumer with the target reason.
# The authority NEVER fails open: a width that no list admits — and an
# order word that is not one of the memory-model orderings — is
# Unsupported by definition.
enum AtomicSupport
  Native
  Emulated
  Unsupported
end

# Is `order_word` one of the memory-model orderings the compiler's
# atomic slice knows (the std::atomic order names — the relaxed/
# acquire/release/acq_rel/seq_cst family)? Any other word makes the
# authority answer Unsupported regardless of the width classes (an
# out-of-domain order is UB per the memory-model docs and never opens
# a width).
def target_atomic_order_word_ok(order_word: String) -> Bool
  order_word == "relaxed" ||
  order_word == "acquire" ||
  order_word == "release" ||
  order_word == "acq_rel" ||
  order_word == "seq_cst"
end

# THE ONE ATOMIC AUTHORITY — the width/op/order question for an atomic
# operation on NORMAL (RAM) memory: Native | Emulated | Unsupported.
# The rule is the description's width classes (the audit item 43 rule:
# native_atomic_widths contains width -> Native; emulated_atomic_widths
# contains width -> Emulated; else Unsupported), gated by the order
# word (a word outside the memory-model orderings is Unsupported no
# matter what the classes say). `op_kind` is the operation-family token
# ("load", "store", "rmw", "cas", "fence" — or an intrinsic family name
# like "fetch_add"/"compare_exchange") carried for the future per-op
# class tables and for the callers' self-documentation; this slice's
# class lists are width-uniform, so op_kind does not change the answer
# (an empty token fails closed — never silently consulted away).
# Consumers on NORMAL memory decide from this answer alone: Native ->
# the instruction forms; Emulated -> the emulation (where the backend
# has one; a desc may admit an emulated class no backend serves yet —
# then the backend keeps its own fail-closed rule naming that gap, as
# backend_rv.tg did for the widths before audit item 43);
# Unsupported -> fail closed. MMIO legality is NOT this function's
# question — MMIO registers are a SEPARATE authority
# (mmio_atomic_support): an emulated sub-word RMW reads and writes the
# whole surrounding word, so a width this function answers Emulated
# for must never be used on an MMIO register, where it could touch a
# NEIGHBOURING register (the RISC-V spec's own note on sub-word
# atomics: the LR/SC emulation of a byte/halfword access operates on
# the containing word, and memory-mapped I/O registers with side
# effects on the neighbouring bytes do not tolerate it).
def atomic_support(desc: TargetDesc, width: Int, op_kind: String, order_word: String) -> AtomicSupport
  if op_kind.len() == 0 then
    return AtomicSupport::Unsupported
  end
  if !target_atomic_order_word_ok(order_word) then
    return AtomicSupport::Unsupported
  end
  if desc.native_atomic_widths.contains(width) then
    return AtomicSupport::Native
  end
  if desc.emulated_atomic_widths.contains(width) then
    return AtomicSupport::Emulated
  end
  AtomicSupport::Unsupported
end

# The SEPARATE MMIO-legality authority — may an atomic operation of
# `width` touch an MMIO register on this target? The MMIO-safe widths
# are mmio_atomic_widths (== the native set on every canonical
# instance: native atomic instructions are single-register accesses;
# an EMULATED sub-word RMW — atomic_support's Emulated answer — reads
# and writes the WHOLE surrounding word and may touch a neighbouring
# register, so it is never MMIO-legal and mmio_atomic_support answers
# Unsupported for it — the emulated widths never appear in
# mmio_atomic_widths by construction). The answer is Native (the
# register access is the width's true single-register instruction) or
# Unsupported (fail closed — MMIO atomics are never emulated, on any
# target, per the RISC-V spec note documented above); Emulated is
# never an MMIO answer. `op_kind` is carried for the future per-op
# tables exactly as on atomic_support (empty -> Unsupported).
def mmio_atomic_support(desc: TargetDesc, width: Int, op_kind: String) -> AtomicSupport
  if op_kind.len() == 0 then
    return AtomicSupport::Unsupported
  end
  if desc.mmio_atomic_widths.contains(width) then
    return AtomicSupport::Native
  end
  AtomicSupport::Unsupported
end

# ———————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————
# VectorSupport — the ONE vector-legality authority (audit item 34)
# ———————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————
# The target side of every v128/v256 vector legality decision the LIR
# vector class and the explicit SIMD intrinsic lowering make (lir.tg's
# vector register class — the `vector_widths`/`vector_lanes` tables —
# and lir_lower_simd_call; the direct emitter's NEON/SSE/AVX arms read
# the same facts through desc.fpu / desc.isa_features). The answers
# over a TargetDesc:
#   V128        — the target carries the baseline 128-bit SIMD facility:
#                 the unified 128-bit vector register file of AArch64
#                 AdvSIMD (Fpu::AdvSimdA64) or the x86-64 SSE2 baseline
#                 (Fpu::Sse2). A 128-bit vector may lower natively.
#   V256        — the target ADDITIONALLY advertises the 256-bit class:
#                 the isa_features "avx"/"avx2" extension tokens. The
#                 Fpu enum has no 256-bit unit and the x86-64 baseline
#                 deliberately assumes no AVX (see x86_64_desc), so a
#                 desc must OPT IN with the token — 256 bits are never
#                 inferred from a 128-bit facility. A 256-bit vector may
#                 lower natively.
#   Unsupported — no modeled facility at the requested width: the
#                 M-profile FPv4-SP/FPv5-D16 units and the RISC-V F/D
#                 units are SCALAR FPUs (FLEN 32/64 — no vector register
#                 file), a 256-bit request on a desc without the AVX
#                 tokens, or any width no descriptor models (512-bit:
#                 no EVEX arm exists). Unsupported = FAIL CLOSED at the
#                 consumer with the target reason; the authority never
#                 fails open.
# The desc carries the SIMD advertisement in TWO fields because the
# architectures spell it differently: aarch64's AdvSIMD IS its FPU (the
# unified v0-v31 file, mandatory ARMv8), while x86-64's SSE2 is the
# required baseline FPU and AVX/AVX2 are per-CPU ISA extensions. No
# consumer re-derives a vector facility from a bare arch test.
enum VectorSupport
  V128
  V256
  Unsupported
end

# Does the desc advertise the baseline 128-bit SIMD facility (the
# AArch64 AdvSIMD file or x86-64 SSE2)?
def target_desc_has_vector_128(desc: TargetDesc) -> Bool
  match desc.fpu
  when Fpu::AdvSimdA64 then true
  when Fpu::Sse2 then true
  when _ then false
  end
end

# Does the desc advertise the 256-bit AVX class (the isa_features
# "avx"/"avx2" tokens)? The x86-64 baseline never assumes AVX, so this
# is an explicit opt-in; no canonical instance carries it this slice.
def target_desc_has_vector_256(desc: TargetDesc) -> Bool
  desc.isa_features.contains("avx") || desc.isa_features.contains("avx2")
end

# THE ONE vector-legality authority: the target's answer for a vector
# of `bits` bits (128/256 are the modeled widths; every other value is
# Unsupported). Consumers: lir.tg's shape gate (vector-local admission),
# lir_lower_simd_call (the per-statement legality gate before a
# LirVecOp statement is produced) and the verifier's route check.
def vector_support(desc: TargetDesc, bits: Int) -> VectorSupport
  if bits == 128 then
    if target_desc_has_vector_128(desc) then VectorSupport::V128 else VectorSupport::Unsupported end
  elsif bits == 256 then
    if target_desc_has_vector_256(desc) then VectorSupport::V256 else VectorSupport::Unsupported end
  else
    VectorSupport::Unsupported
  end
end

# ———————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————
# SyscallAbi — the target's RAW KERNEL-CALL surface (the
# __intrinsic_syscall1..6 family)
# ———————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————
# The target side of the syscall-intrinsic lowering. The answer names
# the desc's INSTRUCTION FORM; the OS (Darwin/Linux) selects the
# number register and the svc immediate INSIDE that form and therefore
# stays a property of the route's AsmTarget (ctx.target.os) — the one
# aarch64 descriptor serves both aarch64 host triples, so the OS can
# never be a second desc field:
#   SyscallA64    — svc: the number in x8 (Linux) / x16 (Darwin), the
#                   arguments in x0..x5, the result in x0.
#   SyscallX86_64 — syscall: the number in rax, the arguments in
#                   rdi/rsi/rdx/r10/r8/r9, the result in rax.
#   SyscallNone   — no modeled kernel-call surface: the syscall
#                   intrinsic fails closed at the LIR lowering with the
#                   target's name (the ARMv7-M and RISC-V descriptors
#                   carry no syscall ABI — a bare-metal kernel is
#                   entered through its own SVC/ECALL convention, which
#                   this compiler does not model). The authority never
#                   fails open: an arch outside the two host families
#                   answers SyscallNone.
enum SyscallAbi
  SyscallA64
  SyscallX86_64
  SyscallNone
end

# THE ONE syscall-surface authority: the desc's instruction form for
# the __intrinsic_syscall* family. Consumers: lir.tg's syscall-call
# recognition (lir_lower_call — SyscallNone fails the call closed with
# the target name), the syscall marker's verifier rule, and the
# emission envelopes (aarch64 via codegen.tg's codegen_syscall, x86-64
# via backend_x86_64.tg's x86_emit_syscall_lir). No consumer re-derives
# a syscall surface from a bare arch test.
def target_desc_syscall_abi(desc: TargetDesc) -> SyscallAbi
  match desc.arch
  when Arch::AArch64 then SyscallAbi::SyscallA64
  when Arch::X86_64 then SyscallAbi::SyscallX86_64
  when _ then SyscallAbi::SyscallNone
  end
end

# ———————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————
# aarch64 (AAPCS64) — Apple Silicon / Linux hosts; the LIR backend's
# target for the audit's current slices (reg_caller_saved/reg_callee_
# saved/arg_regs mirror lir.tg's register model: fixed precolored x0-x7
# = arg_regs, allocatable caller-saved pool x9-x14, scratch x15-x17).
# Atomically, the widths 1/2/4/8 follow asm.tg's target feature table:
# the ARMv8.1 LSE RMW family (LDADDAL/CASAL/SWPAL) is REQUIRED on this
# target — a bare ARMv8.0 core without LSE is not a supported target
# (the LL/SC fallback is a future mode, per that table's note) — and
# the native/emulated/mmio atomic classes are therefore ALL 1/2/4/8
# (nothing is emulated, and the native forms are single-register
# accesses, so the full set is MMIO-safe).
# NOTE: unaligned_policy = Allowed covers NORMAL loads/stores (the
# kernel may trap unaligned in privileged modes); atomic accesses must
# still be naturally aligned on every architecture.
# NOTE: x18 is platform-reserved and never in a register list; x16/x17
# are the intra-procedure-call scratch registers and x8 is the indirect
# result register — all caller-saved scratch, covered by x0-x17. x30
# (LR) is neither caller- nor callee-saved (saved per-call in the
# prologue); x29 (FP) is callee-saved and therefore listed.
# fpu: the base AArch64 FP + AdvSIMD set (the unified v0-v31 register
# file, 128-bit); SVE is a per-core extension and is NOT assumed here.
# memory_spaces: this compiler's aarch64 hosts (darwin/linux) are flat
# address spaces — "flash" applies to a future bare-metal instance.
def aarch64_desc() -> TargetDesc
  TargetDesc {
    name: "aarch64",
    pointer_width: 8,
    endianness: Endianness::Little,
    stack_alignment: 16,
    arch: Arch::AArch64,
    cpu: Cpu::GenericAArch64,
    isa: "aarch64",
    isa_features: Vec::from(["lse"]),
    float_abi: FloatAbi::Hard,
    fpu: Fpu::AdvSimdA64,
    fpu_register_width: 128,
    integer_div: IntegerDiv::Hardware,
    native_atomic_widths: Vec::from([1, 2, 4, 8]),
    emulated_atomic_widths: Vec::new(),
    mmio_atomic_widths: Vec::from([1, 2, 4, 8]),
    unaligned_policy: UnalignedPolicy::Allowed,
    cache_line_size: Option::None,
    red_zone: 128,
    tls_model: "none",
    interrupt_abi: Option::None,
    reg_caller_saved: Vec::from([
      "x0", "x1", "x2", "x3", "x4", "x5", "x6", "x7",
      "x8", "x9", "x10", "x11", "x12", "x13", "x14", "x15",
      "x16", "x17"
    ]),
    reg_callee_saved: Vec::from([
      "x19", "x20", "x21", "x22", "x23", "x24", "x25", "x26",
      "x27", "x28", "x29"
    ]),
    arg_regs: Vec::from(["x0", "x1", "x2", "x3", "x4", "x5", "x6", "x7"]),
    memory_spaces: Vec::from(["ram"])
  }
end

# ———————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————
# The ARMv7-M family — four per-CPU instances replacing the old
# single cortex_m blob (which implied "M3/M4 with VFP"). Every member
# is 32-bit little-endian, stack-align 8, AAPCS32, Thumb-2, with the
# ARMv7-M byte/halfword/word LDREX/STREX exclusives and DMB, hardware
# SDIV/UDIV and hardware MUL. What DIFFERS is the FPU and its ABI:
#   - thumbv7m_cortex_m3 (ARMv7-M, M3): NO FPU — float_abi Soft, and
#     no VFP instruction may be emitted for it (the backend's
#     desc-driven FP gate).
#   - thumbv7em_cortex_m4 (ARMv7-M, M4): NO FPU — the M4's FPU is
#     OPTIONAL, and this instance models the bare M4 (fpu None). A
#     program that needs floats must name the M4F instance.
#   - thumbv7em_cortex_m4f (ARMv7-M, M4F): fpu FPv4SP — the
#     vfpv4-d16 double-register file (d0..d15 / s0..s31) with the
#     single-precision data-processing subset; float_abi Hard —
#     AAPCS32 VFP crossing: float parameters/arguments at FP
#     positions 0..7 ride s0..s15 (d0..d7 for F64), returns in s0/d0.
#     FPv4-SP has NO double-precision data processing: the backend's
#     F64 gates (target_desc_f64_data_processing answers false here)
#     fail F64 arithmetic, F64 compares, F64<->F32 conversions and the
#     int<->F64 conversions closed; the F64 load/store/transfer
#     envelope (VLDR/VSTR/VPUSH/VPOP, the VMOV core<->D and VPUSH/VPOP
#     copies) stays legal. The pre-slice cortex_m instance's behavior
#     (the float route of the Thumb backend) is this instance's
#     behavior: the driver's legacy TANGERINE_LIR_TARGET=cortex-m name
#     maps HERE (the least breaking mapping — the float route requires
#     the FPU).
#   - thumbv7em_cortex_m7 (ARMv7-M, M7): fpu FPv5D16 — the
#     double-precision VFP data-processing variant ("vfp-dp"); the F64
#     data-processing forms of the Thumb backend's float slice are
#     emitted for this instance (the FPv4-SP instance fails them
#     closed — the backend gate reads target_desc_f64_data_processing
#     over this desc, never a hard-coded name); float_abi Hard.
# M0/M0+ (ARMv6-M) is NOT modeled this slice (the audit's M0 note:
# word-only exclusives, no unaligned word loads, 32-cycle MUL,
# divide via RuntimeHelper) — no backend consumes it yet.
# NOTE: atomics must still be naturally aligned even though normal
# word loads/stores tolerate unaligned addresses (unaligned_policy
# Allowed is the NORMAL-access rule).
# interrupt_abi: the shared NVIC record above (armv7m_interrupt_abi).
# memory_spaces ["flash", "ram"] on every member.
# ———————————————————————————————————————————————————————————————

def thumbv7m_cortex_m3_desc() -> TargetDesc
  TargetDesc {
    name: "cortex-m3",
    pointer_width: 4,
    endianness: Endianness::Little,
    stack_alignment: 8,
    arch: Arch::Armv7M,
    cpu: Cpu::CortexM3,
    isa: "armv7-m",
    isa_features: Vec::new(),
    float_abi: FloatAbi::Soft,
    fpu: Fpu::None,
    fpu_register_width: 0,
    integer_div: IntegerDiv::Hardware,
    native_atomic_widths: Vec::from([1, 2, 4]),
    emulated_atomic_widths: Vec::new(),
    mmio_atomic_widths: Vec::from([1, 2, 4]),
    unaligned_policy: UnalignedPolicy::Allowed,
    cache_line_size: Option::None,
    red_zone: 0,
    tls_model: "none",
    interrupt_abi: Option::Some(armv7m_interrupt_abi()),
    reg_caller_saved: Vec::from(["r0", "r1", "r2", "r3", "r12"]),
    reg_callee_saved: Vec::from([
      "r4", "r5", "r6", "r7", "r8", "r9", "r10", "r11"
    ]),
    arg_regs: Vec::from(["r0", "r1", "r2", "r3"]),
    memory_spaces: Vec::from(["flash", "ram"])
  }
end

